  "output": "This incident analysis examines a queue depth spike on [system] that occurred between 09:00 and 09:35 UTC. The incident manifested as an exponential increase in queue depth from baseline 120 items to peak 1,850 items and concurrent task latency degradation from 1.8 seconds to 14.2 seconds median latency over approximately 12 minutes, followed by complete recovery within 11 minutes after rate limit adjustment on an external dependency. This analysis determines the primary triggering event, amplifying mechanisms, and systemic vulnerabilities that enabled the incident.\n\nPrimary Root Cause Analysis:\n\nThe evidence conclusively identifies external dependency B's rate limiting behavior (HTTP 429 responses) as the primary root cause of the queue depth incident. The causality chain is: At 09:15 UTC, dependency B began returning 429 status codes for 31% of outbound requests (E05). This event directly preceded the queue depth plateau at 1,850 items (E02). At 09:35 UTC, when rate limits on dependency B were raised, 429 responses fell below 1% and queue depth returned to below 150 within 11 minutes (E09). This direct correlation between rate limiting behavior and queue depth changes establishes causality.\n\nNo infrastructure changes on [system] occurred that could explain the rate limit (E10), indicating the rate limit was triggered by factors on dependency B's infrastructure outside the [system] control. The rate limit created a scenario where outbound requests failed at high rate, causing upstream accumulation of work items in the queue.\n\nAmplifying Mechanism: Exponential Backoff Retry Policy Failure:\n\nWhile dependency B rate limiting is the primary root cause, the system's exponential backoff retry mechanism acted as a significant amplifier. At 09:16 UTC, retry volume for failed requests increased by 420% compared to baseline, using exponential backoff with maximum three retries per request (E06). This 420% increase in retry traffic directly increased load on the already rate-limited dependency B, creating a positive feedback loop.\n\nEach 429 response triggered exponential backoff retry, which immediately attempted to resend the failed request. Because the underlying cause (dependency B rate limit) was not addressed, each retry also received a 429 response, triggering another backoff cycle. The retry mechanism had no intelligence about rate limiting—it treated 429 responses as transient errors, ensuring retries would continue battering the rate-limited dependency.\n\nDisabling retries temporarily at 09:26 UTC caused queue depth to fall 38% in six minutes while direct failure rate increased (E08). This proves that retries were directly sustaining the queue depth, not protecting against it. The retry mechanism converted a manageable constraint into a cascading failure.\n\nWorker Scaling Ineffectiveness:\n\nAt 09:20 UTC, engineering doubled worker count from 8 to 16 (E07). This yielded only a 6% queue depth reduction over ten minutes—a minimal response that ruled out local worker capacity as the bottleneck.\n\nAnalysis of E04 shows the system was not resource-constrained: CPU utilization 43%, memory 51%, disk I/O 22%, all well below saturation. The system was I/O-bound on external dependency requests, specifically blocked by dependency B's rate limiting. Adding workers to a system blocked by an external rate limit is equivalent to adding checkout lanes when the supply chain is exhausted. Each new worker encounters the same 429 wall from dependency B. Workers accumulate in blocked states without increasing throughput. Queue depth remains high because workers have nowhere productive to send requests.\n\nRate Limit Trigger Analysis:\n\nThe rate limit on dependency B did not arise from malicious behavior. E10 confirms no deploy, schema change, host restart, network route change, or credential rotation occurred during the incident window, eliminating environmental triggers on [system]. The rate limit appears triggered by legitimate load increase on dependency B or by configuration changes on the dependency's infrastructure. At 09:15, dependency B transitioned from accepting all requests to throttling 31%—a step change consistent with reactive rate limiting policies.\n\nSecondary Effects and Cascade:\n\nOnce dependency B 429s began at 09:15, secondary effects cascaded: Retry volume increased 420%, multiplying load back onto the throttled dependency. Task latency increased from 1.8s to 14.2s (E03) due to retry backoff waits and queue accumulation. Queue depth accumulated as tasks waited for retry backoff windows. Higher queue depth created failure perception despite adequate local resources.\n\nRemediation and Resolution:\n\nRaising rate limits on dependency B at 09:35 UTC immediately resolved the incident: 429 responses fell below 1%, queue depth returned to below 150 within 11 minutes, no other system changes were required. This outcome confirms dependency B as root cause and rules out local factors as meaningful contributors. The incident was fully resolved by addressing the root cause rather than scaling or optimizing local systems.\n\nSystem Vulnerabilities Exposed:\n\nThe system lacked a circuit breaker recognizing sustained rate limiting from external dependencies. The retry mechanism had no feedback from 429 responses, treating rate limits identically to transient errors. The incident escalated for 20 minutes before root cause was identified. Auto-scaling on queue depth without dependency health visibility created false remediation while amplifying the underlying problem.\n\nConclusion:\n\nDependency B's rate limiting is the primary root cause. The exponential backoff retry policy is the amplifying mechanism that converted manageable constraint into cascading failure. Local resources remained adequate throughout. Worker scaling proved ineffective due to external bottleneck. The incident was resolved by addressing the root cause, not local remediation. Future prevention requires circuit breaking for rate limiting detection, real-time rate limit monitoring per dependency, and retry policy refinement to prevent load amplification on throttled dependencies.",
